.TH zedit "" "Command"
.SH NAME
zedit \- GUI text editor
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBzedit [\fIoptions\fB] [\fIfile\fB]\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
.HS
zedit -- GUI text editor
Usage:	zedit [ options ] [ file ]
Keys:	^B/^F/^P/^N move, ^A/^E line start/end, ^Z/^V page up/down
	^K kill line, ^W kill selection, ^Y yank, ^S find, ^X^C quit
	ESC s search/replace dialog, ESC r replace this match
	F1 mark, F2 save, F3 open, F4 replace, F5 copy, F6 move,
	F7 find, F8 delete, F9 paste, F10 quit
.HE
.B zedit
is the text editor of the
.SM COHERENT
GUI.
It must be run under the
.B zview
desktop; started elsewhere it exits immediately.
It edits one plain-text file in a resizable window with a scrollbar on
the left edge and a status line showing the file name, a
.B *
when the buffer is modified, and the cursor position as
.IR line , column .
.PP
With a
.I file
argument
.B zedit
loads that file; if it does not exist yet, editing starts in an empty
buffer under that name.
With no argument the buffer starts empty and untitled.
.B zedit
also accepts the standard window
.I options
of GUI applications
(\fB\-T \-I \-S \-P \-H\fR); see
.BR zview .
.PP
The keyboard commands follow the MicroEMACS convention of control keys
and prefix chords.
The C900 cursor keypad works as expected: the desktop translates the
arrow keys and the keypad Home, End, PgUp, PgDn, Del and Enter keys
into the corresponding control keys below; Ctrl held with an arrow
key gives the paging set \- Ctrl+Left/Right = Home/End, Ctrl+Up/Down
= PgUp/PgDn \- and Shift held with one gives the word and buffer
motions \- Shift+Left/Right = word back/forward, Shift+Up/Down =
top/end of file.
.PP
Cursor motion:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fB^B\fR	left one character (wraps to previous line)
	\fB^F\fR	right one character (wraps to next line)
	\fB^P\fR	up one line
	\fB^N\fR	down one line
	\fB^A\fR	to start of line
	\fB^E\fR	to end of line
	\fB^Z\fR	up one page
	\fB^V\fR	down one page
	\fBESC <\fR	to top of file
	\fBESC >\fR	to end of file
	\fBESC f\fR	forward one word
	\fBESC b\fR	back one word
	\fB^L\fR	recentre the cursor line
.DE
Editing:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fBBS\fR	delete left; at column 0, join with previous line
	\fBDEL, ^D\fR	delete right; at end of line, join with next line
	\fB^K\fR	kill to end of line; repeated, appends and takes
		the newline too
	\fBESC d\fR	delete forward through the end of the next word
	\fBESC BS\fR	delete back through the start of the previous word
	\fB^W\fR	kill the selection
	\fB^Y\fR	yank (insert the last killed text)
	\fB^O\fR	open a line below the cursor
	\fB^T\fR	transpose the characters around the cursor
	\fB^G\fR	abort; drop the selection
.DE
Search and file commands:
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fB^S\fR	find next (opens the Search dialog the first time)
	\fBESC s\fR	the Search dialog (always)
	\fBESC r\fR	replace the match under the cursor and step to
		the next, using the dialog's two patterns
	\fB^X^S\fR	save (like F2)
	\fB^X^V\fR	open a file (like F3)
	\fB^X^W\fR	save as: the file-name dialog (like Shift+F2)
	\fB^X^X\fR	swap the cursor and the mark (like Shift+F4)
	\fB^X b\fR	new buffer (like Shift+F3; \fBme\fR's use-buffer)
	\fB^X^U, ^X^L\fR	selection to upper / lower case
	\fBESC c/u/l\fR	Capitalise / UPPER / lower the word at the cursor
	\fBESC !\fR	recentre the cursor line
	\fB^X^C\fR	quit (confirms if the buffer is modified)
.DE
The window menu's
.B Search
entry opens the same dialog: a
.B Find
pattern, a
.B Replace
text, and three verbs.
.B Find
goes to the next match, wrapping past the end of the buffer, and
closes the dialog so the hit can be seen; a match that does not exist
leaves the dialog up saying so.
.B Replace
rewrites the match under the cursor and steps to the next \- so the
first
.B Replace
after opening the dialog only finds a match, and the second changes
it.
.B All
replaces every match from the cursor to the END of the buffer (not the
whole file) and reports the count.
Matching is case-sensitive and a match never spans a newline; a
replacement that would push a line past its stored length is refused
rather than truncated.
.B "ESC r"
does what
.B Replace
does but without the dialog covering the text, which is the way to
work through a buffer match by match.
Function keys, laid out like the Norton Commander editor's key bar
amended (F1 = mark and F4 = replace are swapped from NC \- NC's F1,
help, is the HELP key here \- F3 stays Open, and F9 pastes):
.DS
.ta 6n 18n
	\fBF1\fR	mark: start a keyboard block at the cursor; moving
		the cursor extends it, F1 again freezes it
	\fBF2\fR	save (asks for a name only if untitled)
	\fBF3\fR	open a file
	\fBF4\fR	replace: the Search dialog
	\fBF5\fR	copy the selection to the clipboard
	\fBF6\fR	move: cut the selection to the clipboard
	\fBF7\fR	find next
	\fBF8\fR	delete the selection; with none, ^K^K \- from the
		cursor to the end of the line, then the newline \-
		via the kill buffer, so ^Y puts it back and
		repeated F8s accumulate
	\fBF9\fR	paste the clipboard
	\fBF10\fR	quit (delivered as ^X^C)
	\fBShift+F1\fR	recentre the cursor line
	\fBShift+F2\fR	save as (the file-name dialog)
	\fBShift+F3\fR	new (empty) buffer
	\fBShift+F4\fR	swap the cursor and the mark
	\fBShift+F5\fR	selection to UPPER case
	\fBShift+F6\fR	selection to lower case
	\fBShift+F8\fR	delete word forward
	\fBCtrl+F1\fR	Capitalise the word at the cursor
	\fBCtrl+F4\fR	word to UPPER case
	\fBCtrl+F7\fR	word to lower case
	\fBCtrl+F8\fR	delete word back
	\fBHELP\fR	show the help summary
	\fBCLR/HOME\fR	to top of file (shifted: to end of file)
	\fBSCRN/PRT\fR	recentre the cursor line and repaint (= ^L)
	\fBSTOP\fR	abort; drop the selection
.DE
A block from
.B F1
works with mouse selections' verbs interchangeably: F5/F6/F8 and the
menu act on whichever selection exists.
The remaining shifted and control function keys type
.BR me -only
chords (macros, buffers, window sizes) that
.B zedit
ignores; the full byte map is the
.B zterm
manual page's table.
New is also in the window menu.
Printable characters and TAB insert at the cursor.
Tabs are stored literally and displayed at 8-column stops.
RETURN splits the line at the cursor.
.PP
The mouse places the cursor with a left click, and a left drag selects
text; on release the selection becomes the PRIMARY selection, which a
middle click pastes in any GUI window (at the click point).
Cut and Copy instead use the clipboard, a separate store, so a
clipboard paste and a middle-click paste can carry different text.
.PP
The window menu offers New, Open, Save, Cut, Copy, Paste, Help and
Search above the standard window entries.
Menu Save always shows the file-name dialog, prefilled with the
current name;
.B F2
saves silently under the current name.
In dialogs, TAB cycles among text fields, RETURN fires the default
button and ESC cancels.
.SH FILES
.DS
.ta 8n 32n
	/tmp/.hrclip	clipboard
	/usr/hr/icons/edit.icn	desktop icon
.DE
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR zview ,
.BR zterm ,
.BR zfile ,
.B ed
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All diagnostics appear in dialogs:
`Cannot open that file',
`Cannot write that file',
`Enter a file name',
`Enter text to find',
`Not found',
`Discard unsaved changes?'.
.SH NOTES
The buffer holds at most 1024 lines of at most 240 characters.
Loading stops at the line limit, and longer lines are split; either
event marks the buffer modified so the truncation cannot be saved back
silently.
The kill buffer holds 2048 bytes, a search pattern 31 characters, and
a file name 39 characters.
Every line is written back with a trailing newline.
There is no undo and no replace command.
